[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Unreal Insights and CPU/GPU Profiling in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Performance optimization is a critical phase in game development. Unreal Insights is Unreal Engine's premier profiling system, offering deep visibility into CPU and GPU performance metrics, networking, memory usage, and asset loading. This tutorial covers how to set up, capture, and analyze profiling data using Unreal Insights."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before capturing performance traces, ensure that your project is configured correctly. Tracing can be run on both the Editor and standalone builds, though standalone builds yield the most accurate performance metrics."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Build your target with Development or Shipping configurations (avoid Editor builds for final performance validation)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure the Trace utilities are enabled in your project's Build.cs file if you are writing custom trace channels."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Capturing a Trace Session"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To capture data from a running game instance, you will use console commands to initiate the trace. Launch your packaged game with command-line arguments or enter them into the in-game console."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"open YourMap?resolution=1920x1080 -trace=default,cpu,gpu,loadtime"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Alternatively, you can start and stop tracing dynamically during runtime using the console commands shown below."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"Trace.Start cpu,gpu,loadtime
Trace.Stop"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Analyzing Data in Unreal Insights"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once your session is saved (typically located in the Saved/Profiling/Traces directory), launch the standalone Unreal Insights executable located in your Engine/Binaries/Win64 folder."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"CPU Timings Track: Analyze game thread, render thread, and RHI thread execution times. Look for spikes that exceed your target frame budget (e.g., 16.6ms for 60 FPS)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"GPU Timings Track: Identify heavy rendering passes, costly materials, or excessive dynamic lights impacting the GPU."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Loading Insights: Inspect async loading bottlenecks, shader compilation stalls, and disk I/O performance."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Adding Custom CPU Profiling Scopes"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"To gain deeper visibility into your own C++ code, use the SCOPE_CYCLE_COUNTER or trace macros to mark specific functions and blocks of code for analysis."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"void AMyCharacter::HeavyComputationFunction()
{
    SCOPE_CYCLE_COUNTER(STAT_HeavyComputation);
    // Your expensive calculations here
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering Unreal Insights allows you to systematically track down performance bottlenecks, reduce frame drops, and ensure smooth gameplay experiences across all target hardware platforms."}]
